# Clean Architecture – Layered by Circles

## 1. Enterprise Business Rules (Inner Circle)

**Purpose:** Core, long-term business logic.

**Includes:**

- **Entities**
  - Core domain models
  - Business invariants and rules
  - Independent of application, UI, DB, frameworks

**⚠️ CRITICAL RESTRICTIONS:**
- ❌ **MUST NOT** use Revit API
- ❌ **MUST NOT** reference Revit packages
- ❌ **MUST NOT** know about ViewModels, UI, or frameworks
- ✅ **MUST** be completely framework-agnostic

## 2. Application Business Rules

**Purpose:** Application-specific behavior.

**Includes:**

- **Use Cases**
  - Define application workflows
  - **Rule: Each feature requires a use case**

- **Use Case Interactors**
  - Implement use case logic
  - Orchestrate entities

- **Input Ports**
  - Interfaces used to invoke use cases

- **Output Ports**
  - Interfaces used to return results

**⚠️ CRITICAL RESTRICTIONS:**
- ❌ **MUST NOT** use Revit API
- ❌ **MUST NOT** reference Revit packages
- ❌ **MUST NOT** depend on Infrastructure layer
- ✅ **MUST** depend only on Domain layer
- ✅ **MUST** use only Domain interfaces and entities

## 3. Interface Adapters

**Purpose:** Translate between core logic and external systems.

**Includes:**

- **Controllers**
  - Convert external requests to Input Port models

- **Presenters**
  - Convert Output Port data to UI-friendly models

- **Gateways (Repository Interfaces)**
  - Define data access contracts

- **DTOs / ViewModels**
  - Data structures for communication

## 4. Frameworks & Drivers (Outer Circle)

**Purpose:** Technical and delivery details.

**Includes:**

- **UI**
  - Web, Desktop, Mobile

- **Database**
  - SQL, NoSQL, Files

- **External Systems**
  - APIs, Devices, Third-party services

- **Framework Implementations**
  - ORM, Web frameworks, SDKs

## Interactor Clarification

- **Interactor belongs to the Application Business Rules layer**
- **Implements a single Use Case**

**Depends only on:**
- Entities
- Input / Output Ports

**Must not depend on:**
- UI
- Database
- Frameworks

## CRITICAL: Revit API Restrictions

**⚠️ ABSOLUTE RULE - NEVER VIOLATE:**

- **Domain Layer (Enterprise Business Rules)**
  - ❌ **MUST NOT** use Revit API directly
  - ❌ **MUST NOT** reference Revit API types (Document, Element, etc.)
  - ❌ **MUST NOT** reference Revit packages (Nice3point.Revit.*)
  - ✅ **MUST** use only abstract interfaces defined in Domain
  - ✅ **MUST** be completely independent of Revit

- **UseCases Layer (Application Business Rules)**
  - ❌ **MUST NOT** use Revit API directly
  - ❌ **MUST NOT** reference Revit API types
  - ❌ **MUST NOT** reference Revit packages
  - ✅ **MUST** use only Domain interfaces and entities
  - ✅ **MUST** depend only on Domain layer

- **Infrastructure Layer (Interface Adapters)**
  - ✅ **CAN** use Revit API
  - ✅ **CAN** implement Domain interfaces using Revit API
  - ✅ **CAN** reference Revit packages

**Why:**
- Domain and UseCases must be testable without Revit
- Domain and UseCases must be framework-agnostic
- Only Infrastructure layer knows about Revit implementation details

## Dependency Rule (Global)

- **Dependencies always point inward**
- **Outer layers depend on inner layers**
- **Inner layers never reference outer layers**

## Documentation Rules

- **Document interfaces only**
  - Interfaces require full XML documentation
  - Classes/implementations don't need documentation unless they contain complex logic
  - Don't add unnecessary documentation
